'''Critical geography''' is theoretically informed [[Geography|geographical scholarship]] that promotes&nbsp;[[social justice]],&nbsp;[[Emancipation|liberation]], and&nbsp;[[Left-wing politics|leftist politics]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5gCHckKszz0C&dq=The+dictionary+of+human+geography&pg=PA1974|title=The Dictionary of Human Geography|last1=Gregory|first1=Derek|last2=Johnston|first2=Ron|last3=Pratt|first3=Geraldine|last4=Watts|first4=Michael|last5=Whatmore|first5=Sarah|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|year=2011|isbn=9781444359954|language=en}}</ref> Critical geography is also used as an umbrella term for [[Marxist geography|Marxist]], [[Feminist geography|feminist]], [[Postmodernism|postmodern]], [[Post-structuralism|poststructural]], [[queer]], [[Left-wing politics|left-wing]], and [[Activism|activist]] geography.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://archive.org/details/internationalenc07thri|url-access=limited|title=International Encyclopedia of Human Geography|last=Best|first=U.|publisher=Elsevier|year=2009|isbn=9780080449104|editor-last=Kitchin|editor-first=Rob|location=Oxford|pages=[https://archive.org/details/internationalenc07thri/page/n425 345]–357|editor-last2=Thrift|editor-first2=Nigel}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite journal|last=Castree|first=Noel|year=2000|title=Professionalisation, Activism, and the University: Whither 'Critical Geography'?|journal=Environment and Planning A|language=en|volume=32|issue=6|pages=955–970|doi=10.1068/a3263|doi-access=free}}</ref>

Critical geography is one variant of&nbsp;[[Critical theory|critical social science and the humanities]]&nbsp;that adopts&nbsp;[[Karl Marx|Marx]]’s thesis to interpret and change the world. Fay (1987) defines contemporary critical science as the effort to understand&nbsp;[[oppression]]&nbsp;in a society and use this understanding to promote societal change and liberation.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5K98AAAAIAAJ|title=Critical social science: liberation and its limits|last=Fay|first=Brian|publisher=Cornell University Press|year=1987|isbn=9780801494581|language=en}}</ref> Agger (1998) identifies a number of features of critical social theory practiced in fields like geography, which include: a rejection of&nbsp;[[positivism]]; an endorsement of the possibility of progress; a claim for the structural dynamics of&nbsp;[[Social dominance theory|domination]]; an argument that dominance is derived from forms of false consciousness, ideology, and myth; a faith in the agency of everyday change and self-transformation and an attendant rejection of&nbsp;[[determinism]]; and a rejection of revolutionary expediency.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eB-B6XercZoC|title=Critical Social Theories: An Introduction|last=Agger|first=Ben|publisher=Avalon Publishing|year=1988|isbn=9780813321745|language=en}}{{Dead link|date=December 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

